  <span eza="span_added_for_text:yes;cwidth:0px;;cheight:0px;;wcalc_source:child;wcalc:99px;wocalc:99px;hcalc:1482px;rend_px_area:0;" cwidth="0" style="background-color:transparent;background-size:auto;nodepath:/html/body/div[5]/span[2];pagepos:108;span_added_for_text:yes;cwidth:99;cheight:0px;wcalc_source:child;wcalc:99px;wocalc:99px;hcalc:1482;rend_px_area:0;rcnt:2;ez_min_text_wdth:131;req_px_area:182239.2;obj_px_area:0;req_px_height:20.8;req_margin_and_padding:0;req_ns_height:;vertical_margin:0;parent-line-height:normal;margin-for-scale:0px 0px 0px 0px;padding-for-scale:0px 0px 0px 0px;">
No social issue in America since slavery has ever been so divisive as that of abortion. No issue brings out so much
passion and hatred of the other side. Any court decisions related to abortion regularly bring protestors from both sides.
Polls show Americans are split almost evenly on the issue. Every presidential
election brings out the pro-life side on the right and the pro-choice side on
the left.&nbsp; Abortion often makes it into the debate on other issues.&nbsp;
For example, funding of Planned Parenthood has been discussed in reining in the
out-of-control federal budget.&nbsp; Another example is Obamacare, where final
passage was held up by a few senators who didn't like the abortion language of
the bill.&nbsp; Which side is right? Are there
any shades of gray in this dispute?

