                                <div id="content">
                                    <div class="content-holder">
                                        <div class="blog">
                                            <div class="article">
                                                <p><span id="more-80537"></span><em>Bodily rights arguments for abortion are always extremist arguments, at least in the way people present them. No bodily rights argument that I have ever seen (or even heard of any pro-choice advocate making) leaves room for abortion exceptions.</em></p><div class="insert-post-ads" style="clear:both;float:left;width:100%;margin:0 0 20px 0;"><div id="uds-ads-34" class="widget-1 widget-first widget-odd postadvert hidden-sm hidden-xs widget widget_uds-ads"><p><span class="more-2"><a href="#" class="more">ADVERTISEMENT</a></span></p><div id="uds-ads--1" class="uds-ads  uds-ads-position-3"><div class="uds-ads-251 uds-ad even"><a onclick="trackOutboundLink('http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://www.lifenews.com/index.php?uds_ads_id=3');" href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://www.lifenews.com/index.php?uds_ads_id=3" target="_blank"><img src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://lifenews.wpengine.netdna-cdn.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/onlineforlife10.jpg" alt="Position 9"/></a></div><div class="clear"></div></div></div>
<div id="uds-ads-22" class="widget-2 widget-even postadvert hidden-sm hidden-xs widget widget_uds-ads"><p><span class="more-2"><a href="#" class="more">ADVERTISEMENT</a></span></p><div id="uds-ads--1" class="uds-ads  uds-ads-position-4"><div class="uds-ads-251 uds-ad even"><a onclick="trackOutboundLink('http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://www.lifenews.com/index.php?uds_ads_id=13');" href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://www.lifenews.com/index.php?uds_ads_id=13" target="_blank"><img src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://lifenews.wpengine.netdna-cdn.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/americaneedsfatima13.jpg" alt="Not in use"/></a></div><div class="clear"></div></div></div></div>
<p>Not all pro-choice people are extremists.</p>
<p>A 2013 Gallup poll found that <a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://www.gallup.com/poll/160058/majority-americans-support-roe-wade-decision.aspx">80% of Americans believe abortion should be illegal in the third trimester</a>. A 2012 Lozier Institute poll found that <a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/https://lozierinstitute.org/poll-77-americans-support-ban-sex-selective-abortion/">77% of their respondents believed sex-selective abortions should be against the law</a>. Most people, even pro-choice people, believe there are circumstances when abortion should not be legal.</p>
<p>But almost all pro-choice people use <i>extremist arguments</i>.</p>
<h3><b>What is an extremist argument?</b></h3>
<p>By \xe2\x80\x9cextremist arguments,\xe2\x80\x9d I don\xe2\x80\x99t mean \xe2\x80\x9carguments that extremists often use;\xe2\x80\x9d I mean <b>arguments that necessarily lead to an extremist position. </b>I am not saying that having an extremist position means you must take extremist or violent action. I am just saying if you make an argument that logically requires an extremist position and you don\xe2\x80\x99t take that extremist position, you\xe2\x80\x99re being inconsistent.</p>
<p>For instance, suppose someone said, \xe2\x80\x9cHaving dark skin makes you a non-person, but I really like lots of people with dark skin and I think people ought to be nice to them.\xe2\x80\x9d They\xe2\x80\x99re advocating for being nice, but \xe2\x80\x9chaving dark skin makes you a non-person\xe2\x80\x9d is an extremist argument. The logical conclusion of that argument is that anyone who has dark skin should not be legally protected, that it is morally justified to enslave or kill such people. It doesn\xe2\x80\x99t matter how kind, compassionate, or well-meaning the person is who says it; the argument is extremist.</p>
<p>People are welcome to try to justify abortion with extremist arguments, but they should expect to be gently challenged to hold a consistent view. If you\xe2\x80\x99re making an extremist argument, you should be consistent and hold the extremist view that comes with it.</p>
<p>Pro-life people can make extremist arguments too. For instance, if a pacifist pro-life person said, \xe2\x80\x9cAbortion is wrong because it is always wrong to take any human life,\xe2\x80\x9d then that person would be forced to oppose even killing in self-defense or defense of others. That would be an extremist argument because it necessitates an extremist view.</p>
<p>Describing views (and the arguments that necessitate them) as extremist is intended to be descriptive, not pejorative. Consider the pacifist example. I personally know several extremely virtuous people who have advocated for that view and my intent is not to mock them. It\xe2\x80\x99s also, of course, possible that an extremist view could be true. Describing an argument as extremist doesn\xe2\x80\x99t mean it\xe2\x80\x99s false. I just want people to be consistent. If you use an extremist argument, then accept the implications of it, don\xe2\x80\x99t ignore them because they make you uncomfortable.</p>
<p>Many pro-choice people attempt (unsuccessfully) to paint pro-life claims that unborn babies have a right to live as extremist. The key here is whether the premise <i>must</i> lead to the extremist position. For instance, the claim that it is always wrong to kill innocent human beings does not necessarily lead to the conclusion that the death penalty is immoral. Whatever the correct view is on that issue, it should be obvious that it can be logically consistent to oppose the killing of the <i>innocent</i> and be in favor of the killing of people <i>guilty</i> of murder. Similarly, saying that unborn babies have a right to live does not necessarily lead to a conclusion like, \xe2\x80\x9cWomen should be second-class citizens.\xe2\x80\x9d It can be logically consistent to believe one without believing the other.</p>
<h3>Move Over Personhood, Bodily Rights Is the Reason People Are Pro-Choice</h3>
<p><a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog.jpg"><img class=" wp-image-3727" src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog.jpg" sizes="(max-width: 925px) 100vw, 925px" srcset="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog.jpg 925w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog-300x245.jpg 300w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog-768x628.jpg 768w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog-760x621.jpg 760w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog-489x400.jpg 489w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog-82x67.jpg 82w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0814-Aquinas-for-blog-600x490.jpg 600w" alt="Sarah Wade dialogues in front of ERI's poll table at Aquinas College." width="695" height="568"/></a></p>
<p><b>CLICK LIKE IF YOU&#8217;RE <a href="PRO-LIFE!</b" target="_blankhttp://www.lifenews.com&quot;" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PRO-LIFE</a>!</b></p>
<div id="fb-root"></div>
<p><script type="text/javascript">// <![CDATA[
(function(d, s, id) { var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0]; if (d.getElementById(id)) return; js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id; js.src = "//web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://connect.facebook.net/en_US/all.js#xfbml=1"; fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs); }(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk'));
// ]]&gt;</script></p>
<div class="fb-like" data-href="http://www.facebook.com/lifenews" data-send="true" data-layout="button_count" data-width="450" data-show-faces="false"></div>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Last month ERI had our most recent outreach event at Aquinas College in Michigan. We set up a poll table that asked the question \xe2\x80\x9cShould Abortion Remain Legal?\xe2\x80\x9d and set out pads of paper for students to sign \xe2\x80\x9cYes,\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x80\x9cNo,\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cIt Depends.\xe2\x80\x9d Here are all of the comments, completely unedited, from the students who signed \xe2\x80\x9cYes\xe2\x80\x9d (at least, the ones who left comments):</p>
<ol>
<li>Reproductive Freedom</li>
<li>My uterus pls.</li>
<li>Bodily autonomy</li>
<li>Freedom [unintelligible]</li>
<li>Every case is different</li>
<li>No one can understand the complexity of another\xe2\x80\x99s</li>
<li>My body, my choice/life</li>
<li>Freedom of women\xe2\x80\x99s health &amp; choice</li>
<li>It\xe2\x80\x99s a choice</li>
<li>Just like people cannot be forced to give blood for their children, they should be forced to give life.</li>
<li>I like that ^</li>
<li>It\xe2\x80\x99s the women\xe2\x80\x99s right to choose</li>
<li>Yes!</li>
<li>Motherhood is a choice, I believe in the agency of the person who\xe2\x80\x99s putting their life at risk</li>
<li>Bodily autonomy</li>
<li>Bodily autonomy + viability beginning at birth!</li>
<li>Ban on abortion disproportionately affects poorer communities</li>
<li>None</li>
<li>Not my right to say</li>
<li>Mother\xe2\x80\x99s choice</li>
<li>My body, my choice</li>
<li>\xe2\x80\x9cChoice\xe2\x80\x9d</li>
<li>Not your body, my body</li>
<li>My choice</li>
<li>Keep it clean + safe.</li>
<li>Making them illegall won\xe2\x80\x99t stop them, just lead to more deaths. Keep it clean + safe.</li>
<li>Serious women\xe2\x80\x99s health issue women should be able to choose how to use their body</li>
<li>Everyone has a reason, + we are not one to judge bodily autonomy</li>
<li>If it\xe2\x80\x99s not legal, people will do it anyway</li>
<li>It\xe2\x80\x99s a woman\xe2\x80\x99s own body</li>
<li>My vagina my right</li>
</ol>
<p>For those of you keeping score, that\xe2\x80\x99s fourteen out of thirty-one comments with explicit references to bodily autonomy, six that vaguely point to \xe2\x80\x9cchoice\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cfreedom,\xe2\x80\x9d three concerned about illegal abortions, three that don\xe2\x80\x99t think it\xe2\x80\x99s their right to judge another person\xe2\x80\x99s situation, one vague \xe2\x80\x9cYes!\xe2\x80\x9d, one vague \xe2\x80\x9cNone,\xe2\x80\x9d one \xe2\x80\x9cMotherhood is a choice, I believe in the agency of the person who\xe2\x80\x99s putting their life at risk,\xe2\x80\x9d and one \xe2\x80\x9cBan on abortion disproportionately affects poorer communities.\xe2\x80\x9d Those last two are different enough that I don\xe2\x80\x99t want to lump them in with the others.</p>
<p>Notice the number of statements about the belief that the unborn is not a human person: <strong>zero</strong>. Our polls aren\xe2\x80\x99t scientific, but every time we do them the results look just like this. People do justify their views about abortion by arguing against the humanity of the unborn, so we still need to talk about that, but I don\xe2\x80\x99t think it\xe2\x80\x99s the driving force behind <i>why</i> most people are pro-choice. The more I talk with pro-choice students, the more convinced I become that at the end of the day, they are pro-choice because of bodily rights. If you are not prepared to <a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/dfg/">recognize and engage bodily rights arguments in a persuasive way</a>, then you shouldn\xe2\x80\x99t be surprised on the occasions when you don\xe2\x80\x99t change pro-choice minds.</p>
<h3>Why Bodily Rights Arguments for Abortion Logically Necessitate Extremism</h3>
<p>Bodily rights arguments for abortion are always extremist arguments, at least in the way people present them. No bodily rights argument that I have ever seen (or even heard of any pro-choice advocate making) leaves room for exceptions. <b>But the majority of these people believe there should be times when abortion should be illegal.</b></p>
<p>Almost all pro-choice people make bodily rights arguments, but most of these people, well-meaning or not, are flagrantly inconsistent.[<a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Almost%20all%20pro%2Dchoice%20people%20make%20bodily%20rights%20arguments%2C%20but%20most%20of%20these%20people%20are%20flagrantly%20inconsistent%3A%20http://bit.ly/21tvvAT" target="_blank">Tweet that</a>]\xc2\xa0Usually there\xe2\x80\x99s a stage in pregnancy they\xe2\x80\x99re uncomfortable with, and often there are reasons for abortion that they don\xe2\x80\x99t agree with. I frequently hear, \xe2\x80\x9cYou shouldn\xe2\x80\x99t be able to have an abortion for sex-selection,\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cThere should be a limit on how many abortions someone can get,\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cThey shouldn\xe2\x80\x99t be able to use it for birth control.\xe2\x80\x9d</p>
<p>This is clearly not a well-thought out position. It\xe2\x80\x99s the position of someone that has some of their moral intuitions intact, like about how awful late-term abortion is, but they haven\xe2\x80\x99t thought beyond the talking point they\xe2\x80\x99re using to justify other abortions.</p>
<p>There is no way to consistently argue that a) first trimester abortions should be legal because \xe2\x80\x9cit\xe2\x80\x99s a woman\xe2\x80\x99s right to decide what to do with her body,\xe2\x80\x9d AND b) second-trimester abortions should <i>not</i> be legal. <strong>If the thing that justifies abortion in the first trimester is women\xe2\x80\x99s bodily autonomy, then abortion continues to be justified in the second and third trimester because the fetus still resides inside her body.</strong> The same principle applies to distasteful reasons for abortions. You may not like sex-selective abortions, but you can\xe2\x80\x99t oppose them while simultaneously supporting early abortions with bodily rights arguments.</p>
<p><a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place.jpg"><img class="alignnone wp-image-3736" src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place.jpg" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" srcset="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place.jpg 1200w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place-300x200.jpg 300w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place-768x511.jpg 768w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place-1024x681.jpg 1024w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place-760x505.jpg 760w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place-518x344.jpg 518w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place-250x166.jpg 250w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place-82x55.jpg 82w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/rock-hard-place-600x399.jpg 600w" alt="Between a rock and a hard place" width="665" height="442"/></a></p>
<p><a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://spot.colorado.edu/%7Eheathwoo/Phil160,Fall02/thomson.htm">The violinist argument</a> falls into the same problem. If you\xe2\x80\x99re justifying abortion by arguing that women have the right to abort just like you have the right to unplug from a violinist, then you can\xe2\x80\x99t make exceptions. If I want to unplug from the violinist because the violinist is African-American and I\xe2\x80\x99m a racist, I still have the right to unplug. If I want to unplug eight months into the procedure, I still have the right to unplug. Similarly, if someone wants an abortion at eight months, or because of sex-selection or even racism, you can\xe2\x80\x99t deny it to her if you\xe2\x80\x99re justifying the other abortions through bodily autonomy.</p>
<p>The only reasonable way to justify a pro-choice position with exceptions is to argue that the unborn is not a human person early in pregnancy but becomes a person later in pregnancy. I\xe2\x80\x99m not saying that\xe2\x80\x99s a good position, but it can at least be logically consistent. If you become a person when you can feel pain, then it makes sense to oppose abortion after a fetus can feel pain. The problem comes if you are justifying abortion by making a bodily rights argument. As soon as you do that, you have thrown open the door to accepting all abortions, and the only question is whether you\xe2\x80\x99re consistent enough to recognize it.</p>
<h3><b>How to Turn the Tables</b></h3>
<p>In an earlier blog post, I described <a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/four-practical-dialogue-tips-from-my-conversation-with-brent/">how powerful it is to turn the tables on pro-choice rhetoric</a>. This is another great example of a time to do that. I don\xe2\x80\x99t have a good example of a specific story where I did this, so what follows is merely an estimation of the type of conversation I\xe2\x80\x99ve had many times. This is just for the sake of illustrating the idea, these are not direct quotations from a specific conversation:</p>
<p>Tim: Why are you pro-choice?</p>
<p>Jane: <b>My body, my choice. It\xe2\x80\x99s nobody else\xe2\x80\x99s business what I do with my own body.</b></p>
<p>Tim: I\xe2\x80\x99d like to understand your view better. Do you think there are any circumstances when a woman shouldn\xe2\x80\x99t be allowed to get an abortion?</p>
<p>Jane: What do you mean?</p>
<p>Tim: I\xe2\x80\x99ll give you a common example. Most people are pretty uncomfortable with abortions at like, eight months. What do you think?</p>
<p><a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/20week.jpg"><img class="wp-image-3737 alignright" src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/20week.jpg" sizes="(max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px" srcset="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/20week.jpg 400w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/20week-245x300.jpg 245w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/20week-327x400.jpg 327w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/20week-82x100.jpg 82w" alt="20-week fetus." width="200" height="245"/></a>Jane: Oh, I\xe2\x80\x99m definitely not for that. I\xe2\x80\x99m only for abortion in the first twenty weeks.</p>
<p>Tim: Really? I\xe2\x80\x99m confused. <b>At twenty-two weeks, isn\xe2\x80\x99t it her body, her choice? How is it anyone else\xe2\x80\x99s business what she does with her own body?</b></p>
<p>Jane: Well, I get what you mean, but after twenty weeks it\xe2\x80\x99s too late. The baby is too developed.</p>
<p>Tim: I want to understand your view but I\xe2\x80\x99m struggling. Based on what you just said, it seems like you aren\xe2\x80\x99t really justifying abortion with bodily rights. It seems like you\xe2\x80\x99re justifying abortion by claiming that a fetus doesn\xe2\x80\x99t count as a human person until twenty weeks. The bodily rights claim isn\xe2\x80\x99t sufficient by itself to justify abortion because as soon as you think the fetus is human, you don\xe2\x80\x99t think abortion is justified.</p>
<p>Jane: Yeah, I think both at the same time. In the first twenty weeks it isn\xe2\x80\x99t a person, AND women should have the rights over their bodies.</p>
<p>Tim: I actually think you don\xe2\x80\x99t need the bodily rights argument. If the fetus isn\xe2\x80\x99t a person, I think abortion is obviously justified, don\xe2\x80\x99t you?</p>
<p>Jane: Yeah, I guess so. Bodily autonomy just matters a lot to me.</p>
<p>Tim: That makes sense. I think bodily autonomy is really important too. It seems like our main disagreement isn\xe2\x80\x99t about bodily autonomy though, it seems like we need to determine if the fetus is a human person.</p>
<p><strong>Notice how vacuous the pro-choice rhetoric is if they have exceptions.</strong> \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s nobody else\xe2\x80\x99s business what I do with my own body (but only in the circumstances I\xe2\x80\x99m comfortable with)!\xe2\x80\x9d</p>
<p>You should also notice that the extremely common arguments about back-alley abortions have the exact same problem. If you\xe2\x80\x99re going to justify first-trimester abortion by arguing that it\xe2\x80\x99s better to keep abortions legal so women don\xe2\x80\x99t seek them illegally, then how can you justify making <i>any</i> abortion illegal? If you make an abortion illegal, a woman might seek it anyway and do it unsafely.</p>
<p>When people make extremist arguments, like bodily rights arguments and back-alley abortion arguments, I\xe2\x80\x99m not recommending that you treat them rudely or call them names. I\xe2\x80\x99m not saying to scream at them that they\xe2\x80\x99re extremists. Please don\xe2\x80\x99t do that. I\xe2\x80\x99m suggesting that you help them to understand the logic of their argument. Help them to see that they only have two options: either 1) embrace the extremist position, or 2) reject the extremist argument.</p>
<p><a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/options-bodily-rights.jpg"><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-3739" src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/options-bodily-rights.jpg" sizes="(max-width: 495px) 100vw, 495px" srcset="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/options-bodily-rights.jpg 495w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/options-bodily-rights-208x300.jpg 208w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/options-bodily-rights-278x400.jpg 278w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/options-bodily-rights-82x118.jpg 82w" alt="options-bodily-rights" width="495" height="713"/></a></p>
<h3>But What If We Make People Even More Pro-Choice?</h3>
<p>There is a clear risk to this strategy. Sometimes they\xe2\x80\x99ll just embrace their extremism and then they\xe2\x80\x99ll walk away even more pro-choice than before! My colleague Jacob Nels shared this story with me of a conversation he had at our other recent outreach day in Michigan, this time at Grand Valley State University.</p>
<p><a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog.jpg"><img class="wp-image-3721 " src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog.jpg" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" srcset="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog.jpg 1200w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog-300x240.jpg 300w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog-768x614.jpg 768w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog-1024x819.jpg 1024w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog-760x608.jpg 760w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog-500x400.jpg 500w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog-82x66.jpg 82w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/IMG_0702-1200-for-blog-600x480.jpg 600w" alt="Jacob Nels speaking to Grand Valley State professor about bodily rights." width="698" height="559"/></a></p>
<p>I engaged a young female professor in a dialogue by asking her:</p>
<p>Jacob: Excuse me, ma\xe2\x80\x99am. Would you take a moment to sign our poll? Should 20-week abortions be legal?</p>
<p>She signed: No.</p>
<p>Jacob: Thanks for signing our poll. Would you mind telling me why you answered no?</p>
<p>Professor: Once it goes that far along, abortions shouldn\xe2\x80\x99t happen.</p>
<p>Jacob: Okay. Would you help me understand where the line is for you and why?</p>
<p>I showed her fetal development pictures in the ERI brochure.</p>
<p><img class="wp-image-3716 " src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages.png" sizes="(max-width: 1197px) 100vw, 1197px" srcset="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages.png 1197w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages-300x235.png 300w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages-768x602.png 768w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages-1024x803.png 1024w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages-760x596.png 760w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages-510x400.png 510w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages-82x64.png 82w, http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-Fetal-Development-Pages-600x471.png 600w" alt="These are the first two pages on the inside of the ERI Outreach Brochure." width="682" height="535"/></p>
<p>Jacob: Could you point out when in human development abortion is not okay?</p>
<p>Professor: I\xe2\x80\x99m not really sure.</p>
<p>After a few more minutes of conversation, she said:</p>
<p>Professor: Women should have the right to do what they want with their body.</p>
<p>Jacob: It sounds like you are saying that you believe women should have a right to complete bodily autonomy, and you do still believe the unborn is a human person. So, is it your belief that a woman\xe2\x80\x99s right to bodily autonomy supersedes the life of the fetus?</p>
<p>Professor: Yes.</p>
<p>Jacob: Could you clarify that for me? It seems like your statement that women should have complete bodily autonomy is in direct conflict with your answer to our poll that 20-week abortions should not be legal. Have you ever seen a picture of a 20-week abortion?</p>
<p>Professor: No, I haven\xe2\x80\x99t.</p>
<p>Jacob: I have one here. May I show it to you?</p>
<p>She agreed and looked at the picture. <a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://blog.equalrightsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/ERI-Brochure-20-Week-Abortion-Pages.png" target="_blank">Click here</a> to see the page Jacob showed her from our brochure. Warning: It is extremely graphic.</p>
<p>Professor: Oh, this is really bad!</p>
<p>Jacob: Yeah it is, but in order to be consistent with your belief in bodily autonomy, then this procedure would have to be okay.</p>
<p>Professor: Yeah, I guess you\xe2\x80\x99re right. I have to get going to class, but can I change my vote?</p>
<p>Jacob\xe2\x80\x99s heart sank as he watched her switch her vote from \xe2\x80\x9cNo\xe2\x80\x9d to \xe2\x80\x9cYes \xe2\x80\x93 20-week abortions should be legal.\xe2\x80\x9d She left and didn\xe2\x80\x99t come back.</p>
<p>So Jacob reasonably asks, \xe2\x80\x9cIsn\xe2\x80\x99t this a dangerous approach? Aren\xe2\x80\x99t we going to help some people become <i>more </i>pro-choice?\xe2\x80\x9d</p>
<p>I have run into the same problem when I\xe2\x80\x99ve responded to pro-choice arguments for personhood by pointing out that the other person\xe2\x80\x99s understanding of personhood would either include animals like squirrels or exclude newborn humans. Sometimes they react by abandoning their pro-choice argument but other times they double down and say, \xe2\x80\x9cFine! Then it\xe2\x80\x99s okay to kill newborns,\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cFine! Then squirrels are people too.\xe2\x80\x9d</p>
<p>When people react that way, in one sense it is like they become more pro-choice. But they won\xe2\x80\x99t be more <i>comfortably pro-choice</i>. There is a type of pro-choice view that many pro-choice people hold. It\xe2\x80\x99s a view that feels really moderate, reasonable, and compassionate. They know they\xe2\x80\x99re uncomfortable with late-term abortion, but they also know they want first trimester abortions legal so they just start believing both things. They get comfortable with these beliefs.</p>
<p><b>My goal is to graciously drive the comfortable pro-choice position into extinction.</b></p>
<p>It is frustrating to see people bite the bullet on awful, extremist views. I have watched pro-choice students go as far as to say that parents should have the legal right to kill their ten-year-olds, because that was what it took for them to remain pro-choice about abortion. <i>But I actually think that leaves them better off.</i> I would rather they walk away knowing that they had to agree to something truly horrific in order to remain pro-choice. One of the best ways to help someone to change her mind about abortion is to make her experience cognitive dissonance.</p>
<p>If a person\xe2\x80\x99s only options are 1) pro-life or 2) horrifically pro-choice, most people have enough decency to eventually choose the former. I\xe2\x80\x99m cautiously optimistic that many of the college students I have seen agree to awful conclusions will grow up eventually and recognize that squirrels aren\xe2\x80\x99t people, rape is actually wrong (not just distasteful), and that a newborn child has the right to live. Some won\xe2\x80\x99t, but I\xe2\x80\x99m honestly more interested in pulling the moderates to our side than I am worried about helping the most hardcore pro-choice people become even more extreme.</p>
<p>If thousands of comfortable pro-choice people have to choose between the extremism that their arguments demand and the voices of their consciences, then many of them will become pro-life.</p>
<p><a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331/http://lifenews.wpengine.netdna-cdn.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/protest20b.jpg"><img class="alignnone size-full wp-image-80538" src="http://web.archive.org/web/20160507122331im_/http://lifenews.wpengine.netdna-cdn.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/protest20b.jpg" alt="protest20b" width="599" height="403"/></a></p>
